7. Monitoring Targets

Atlas recognizes the following monitoring targets.

Target	Description
Workflow Definition	Published workflow metadata
Workflow Instance	Runtime execution
Workflow Program	Coordinated execution
Activities	Individual work units
Participants	Human, AI, and systems
Resources	Execution resources
Events	Canonical Events
Execution Environment	Infrastructure context
8. Telemetry

Monitoring SHALL collect telemetry including:

Execution duration
State transitions
Activity completion
Waiting times
Queue lengths
Resource utilization
Policy evaluations
Generated events

Telemetry SHALL remain versioned and timestamped.

9. Health Assessment

Workflow health SHALL evaluate:

Execution progress
Resource availability
Policy compliance
Activity success rate
Dependency status
Participant availability
Error frequency
Recovery effectiveness

Atlas defines canonical health states.

State	Description
Healthy	Operating normally
Degraded	Reduced capability
At Risk	Significant operational concern
Critical	Immediate intervention required
Unknown	Health cannot be determined

Health SHALL be continuously reassessed.

10. Performance Monitoring

Monitoring SHALL support metrics including:

Workflow duration
Activity duration
Throughput
Latency
Resource efficiency
Completion rate
Recovery rate
SLA/SLO compliance

Performance metrics SHALL remain independently reproducible.

11. Alerts

Alerts MAY be generated for:

Workflow failure
SLA violation
Timeout
Resource exhaustion
Policy violation
Health degradation
Security incident
Operational anomaly

Every alert SHALL define:

Alert ID
Severity
Trigger condition
Affected workflow
Recommended response
12. Operational Dashboards

Dashboards MAY visualize:

Workflow status
Program progress
Active workflows
Pending approvals
Resource utilization
Health indicators
Alert summaries
Historical trends

Dashboards SHALL present observational information only.

13. Anomaly Detection

Monitoring MAY identify anomalies including:

Unexpected execution duration
Abnormal activity frequency
Repeated failures
Resource bottlenecks
Unusual participant behavior
Event sequence deviations
Recovery loops

Anomalies SHALL generate Canonical Events where appropriate.

14. Monitoring Governance

Every monitoring implementation SHALL define:

Monitoring authority
Observation scope
Data retention policy
Access permissions
Applicable policies
Audit requirements

Monitoring governance SHALL remain independently auditable.

15. Monitoring Quality

Every monitoring implementation SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Timeliness
Completeness
Explainability
Traceability
Non-intrusiveness
Scalability
Governance compliance

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

16. Conformance

A Workflow Monitoring implementation conforms if it:

Observes canonical workflow executions.
Collects governed telemetry.
Supports health assessment.
Generates standardized alerts.
Preserves execution traceability.
Maintains non-intrusive observation.
Supports governance requirements.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted anomaly detection.
Predictive workflow monitoring.
Digital Twin observability.
Federated monitoring networks.
Autonomous operational diagnostics.
Machine-verifiable telemetry integrity.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical monitoring semantics.

